UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 2: Texture rubbings and pattern designs', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A rubbing captures a texture by placing thin paper over a rough surface and rubbing the side of a crayon or soft pencil across it. The raised parts print dark and the dips stay light. Hold the paper still and rub in one direction. You can cut up rubbings and arrange them, or repeat a small shape called a motif in rows to make a pattern design. Plan where each texture will go before you stick it.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou have rubbings of a doormat, a tile and a leaf. Plan a repeating pattern design for a bookmark that uses all three. Say how you will repeat the motif and which texture goes where.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''I will cut the leaf into small diamonds and repeat them down both edges, put tile squares in the middle and use the doormat strip at the top and bottom.'' Repeating one motif and keeping the textures in separate areas makes a clear, balanced design.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that pressing hard makes a better rubbing. Pressing too hard tears the paper and blurs the texture, so rub lightly with the side of the crayon and hold the paper steady.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCollect four textures from around your home and make a rubbing of each with a crayon. Cut them up and design a repeating pattern on a card, labelling the motif and the number of times it repeats. Keep all scraps in a small tray so they do not blow around.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and 