vidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Sia makes rubbings of a leaf, a coin and a brick wall. She cuts the leaf rubbing into small squares and repeats them in rows to make a border, with a coin circle in the centre. The repeating motif makes a border pattern and the three rubbings give three different textures.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYou have rubbings of a doormat, a tile and a leaf. Plan a repeating pattern design for a bookmark that uses all three. Say how you will repeat the motif and which texture goes where.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''I will cut the leaf into small diamonds and repeat them down both edges, put tile squares in the middle and use the doormat strip at the top and bottom.'' Repeating one motif and keeping the textures in separate areas makes a clear, balanced design.","explain":"A rubbing captures a texture by placing thin paper over a rough surface and rubbing the side of a crayon or soft pencil across it. The raised parts print dark and the dips stay light. Hold the paper still and rub in one direction. You can cut up rubbings and arrange them, or repeat a small shape called a motif in rows to make a pattern design. Plan where each texture will go before you stick it.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that pressing hard makes a better rubbing. 